  { q: 'What is the difference between Myers diff and patience diff?', a: 'Both decide which lines changed. Myers diff, the Git default introduced by Eugene Myers in 1986, minimizes the total added and removed lines and is fast and correct. Patience diff anchors on unique lines first, producing more readable output for large refactors. Both give valid results; the difference is readability for complex changes.' },
  { q: 'Why does my diff show every line as changed?', a: 'The usual cause is invisible characters, most often line endings. Windows ends lines with a carriage return and line feed, while Linux and macOS use a line feed alone, so a file re-saved elsewhere changes every line. Normalize the line endings or ignore whitespace, and the Best Answer Hub Diff Checker will show only the real changes.' },
  { q: 'How do I ignore whitespace differences when comparing?', a: 'Whitespace differences, such as trailing spaces or tabs versus spaces, are invisible on screen but count as changes. The fix is to normalize both sides to the same spacing and line endings before comparing. Because the Best Answer Hub Diff Checker compares exactly what is present, cleaning the whitespace first gives the clearest result.' },
